I teach a course called religion and  sexuality which I had intended to be a  small seminar but has ended up being a  slightly larger course I teach every two  years and enrolls between 80 and 120  students and the first day I explain the  themes of the course essentially as a  series of way of encouragement that you  leave if you're uncomfortable with so I  say I think you should probably leave if  you really don't like religion I think  you should probably leave if you really  really don't like sex and third I think  you should leave if you're going to be  incredibly uncomfortable with ambiguity  and I do that both because it's a  shopping period class and I want  students to feel like they're getting  the class that they expect but also  because to me the entire point of the  course is to subvert the things that  they normally think about those  categories imagining that religions are  all awful orthodoxies that keep people  from their freedom and that sex is  always a source of freedom these are  things that I want them to think about  and come back to after reading a lot of  material on American religious history  and particularly the way religious  movements prescribed particular sexual  behavior but also the way that the  broader culture has in it various sexual  acts promoted talked about prescribed  that themselves have a kind of ritual  aspect when I was originally planning  the course and thinking of it as a  seminar I did a lot of research of  primary documents in American religion  where their explicit sexual  prescriptions and the person that I  first researched was a man named John  Humphrey Noyes who is most famous in the  broader culture for coining the term  free love but within the American  religious history is known as somebody  who was obsessed with the idea of  perfection that human beings could be  rid of sin and he was particularly  interested in the idea that he's sourced  in the New Testament where Jesus says  that in heaven there will be no marriage  and he decided that he believes really  essential practice for all human beings  would be a sort of notion of complex  marriage in which to speak kind of  abbreviated about very complicated  system we were all essentially married  to one another and I've always known  about John Humphrey Noyes  I'd always known about the United  perfectionist  that he led in upstate New York but I  had never understood that this  revelation occurred to him while he was  at Yale and that got me to thinking as I  was reading a lot of articles in The New  York Times and other paper is about Yale  graduates and what they were doing after  graduation and I started thinking for  all the amazing things that Yale  graduates do and they do so many amazing  things I would like to hear more of them  leading Brigades of new sexual  revolution and thinking through what  kind of lives we live in this present  culture as sexual beings and what really  excited me to discover this about noise  was to imagine teaching a course that  got students to think of themselves as  leaders not only of politics and  economics but also of sexual  consciousness in the course we have a  couple different kinds of assignments  the first one this semester that the  students are actually writing right now  is they have to write a response to  young man's memoir and polemic young  recent graduate of Yale who wrote a book  called sexy God at Yale where he wrote a  pretty sharp critique of what he felt  was a kind of excessive sexual culture  among the undergraduates here and I'm  kind of failure of intellectual and  moral discipline towards their sexual  selves and right now the students are  writing they've read some of hardens  book and I've invited them to write a  letter to the editor about this book not  literally to send it off unless they so  choose but to try to create an argument  about how they think about their sexual  culture at Yale relative to hardness  readings other assignments in the class  include asking them to write a speech  for Michelle Obama using the writings of  Michelle Foucault to think through about  the nature of the body and how important  the body is for the National politic  this is in response to Michelle Obama's  let's move campaign they're also going  to be asked to write a nomination for a  text to be used in the course something  from popular culture or from American  religious history where they find a  religious group and research their  prescriptions for sexual action all of  these assignments show that my intention  is to try to draw in students who are  interested in getting an introduction to  the humanities we talk a lot in the  course about basic acts of argumentation  and close reading but also to try to  pull from as many disciplines as  Cybil and to not imagine that they are  coming in and must know anything in  advance of the course about the history  of sexuality about the history of  America or about the history of gender  what I want them to do is to come in and  think that they themselves have  resources to call upon their own  experiences and perceptions and while I  discourage first-person talk in class  what I encourage is using all that  first-person passionate about what  they've perceived in their worlds to  come and bear on these texts in the  class I've had everything from football  players to powerful queer activists  there's a wide array and I think that  they're brought to the course precisely  because the course does not take any  position other than you yourself have  something to say about the discomfort  you have about the sexual culture you  occupy and I found in teaching it here  that over and over again students come  to understand and be able to name what  they find so structuring and limiting  about American sexual culture and I hope  they then lead new rebellions about ways  we could live within it  as an LGBT person myself I'm acutely  acutely sensitive to the way that issues  of sexual concern sexual crisis sexual  politics often seem in the public sphere  to be dominated by LGBT people that the  question of sexual suffering or sexual  confusion seems to be largely a desire  to manage the LGBT population I've tried  to design this course in a way that  emphasizes its inclusivity insofar as I  want to have people in the room who come  in imagining themselves to be any number  of sexual identities and what I like  them to come out as is not the bunked of  those identities but to imagine that the  boundaries of those conceptions have  been designed by someone else and that  what we have to try to do is to bring  our own consciousness to bear upon our  experiences and to do that with as much  care and concern for the history that  brings us to this present one of my  great excitements about the course is  when I have a student come into my  office who says that they came because  they're a religious Studies major and  that this fill the requirements or that  they just thought it would be a fun way  to fill up kind of introductory  humanities credit and that they say that  over the course the class that they came  to realize how  much they see in the media about LGBT  people about women's rights are really  caricatures and caricatures invented to  propound a certain form of ideal  sexuality they made them realize how  much of their life was itself a kind of  assignment an assignment to be a kind of  person that they didn't feel themselves  to be and even as they may go on to  study something far afield from the  study of religion and sexuality they  will continue to hold that inside  themselves as something that guides them  not only as scholars or as professionals  but also as human beings trying to  fulfill their greatest purpose
 

